WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:03.340
This is the Technical Difficulties.
We're playing Citation Needed.

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.560
Joining me in the studio today:
he reads books, you know — it's Chris Joel!

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.640
Hellooo.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.340
Everybody's favourite Gary Brannan — Gary Brannan!

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.260
Salutem.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:10.900
And the bounciest man on the internet — Matt Gray!

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.760
It's not a studio, it's a kitchen diner.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:14.900
I'm choosing to quietly ignore that.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.860
In front of me I have an article from Wikipedia,
and these guys can't see it.

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:21.420
Every fact they get right is a point and a ding [DING],

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.180
and there's a special prize for
particularly good answers, which is:

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:28.780
Today's article is: Lisa Clayton.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:30.840
She's from somewhere that makes bricks.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.740
Ooh. Why do you say that?

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:33.780
ALL: Clayton.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.060
TOM: Ohh, right.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:36.360
Erm, Birmingham actually.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.780
GARY: Yep.
MATT: Yup.

00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:38.660
[Laughter]

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.660
Okay, so someone from Birmingham.
What is this, f***ing Crime Watch?

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.320
CHRIS: 'Have you seen Lisa Clayton?'

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.980
'Have you seen her? Do you know her?'

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.200
Well, here's the thing: Lisa Clayton,
you wouldn't have seen her for quite a while...

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.160
For 285 days.

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:54.440
Because she was invisible?

00:00:54.440 --> 00:00:56.060
[Laughter]
Briefly.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:58.020
That's not briefly!

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:00.700
You'd cause bedlam with 285 days of invisibility.

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:02.900
It's brief on the universal scale!

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.480
Oh, yes, yes.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.840
So 285 days of being invisible.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:09.420
Er, of not being seen by the world.

00:01:09.580 --> 00:01:10.460
Hibernation.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:11.540
In a cupboard.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:13.320
World Hide and Seek champion.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.440
[Laughter]

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:15.840
I'd go with that one.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.520
Er, not quite. She did certainly go away for a while.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.260
Arctic wanderer.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.020
Ooh, explorer certainly.
I will give you a point for that. [DING]

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:26.200
Explorer in the sense of going places
rather than the sense of discovering places.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.400
Did she go to Wolverhampton?

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.300
That's still not been discovered!

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:32.940
The mystical city of...

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.200
ALL: Wolverhampton!

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:36.120
MATT: Eugh.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.580
The one thing I do know about Wolverhampton,
they've got battered chips.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:42.760
TOM and MATT: Oooh.
GARY: Eh?

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.040
Two very different reactions there. I went,
[Doubtful]  'Oooh...'

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.840
Matt went, 'Yep, yep, I'll have that.'

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.460
Yeah... I'm with Matt on this.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.860
See, it would involve going to Wolverhampton to get it,

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:54.460
but what I can do is try it at home.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.300
The other thing is, I think you'd only need three.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:01:59.680
I think after three, I'd have had enough.

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.420
Have you ever had a battered chocolate bar?

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:02.240
ALL: No.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:07.220
I once tried that, but I didn't work out you had to put batter in it. So I just dropped a Rocky into a pot of oil.

00:02:10.420 --> 00:02:12.260
CHRIS: Fwoomf! 'Awww.'

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:16.920
There's a chippie round the back of the Nestlé factory,
and I had a battered Yorkie.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:18.120
Oh... Yeah... No.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.280
TOM: Ohh, that's a dense...

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:22.260
One bite was enough.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.320
Because it is just hot dripping chocolate,
hot dripping oil, and... [Gags]

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.200
It's just a weapon, isn't it, basically.

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.460
We're back to our chocolate rods from God...

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.620
GARY: We are.
MATT and TOM: Yeah.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:33.600
That's how you get the casing on
to get it through the atmosphere.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:35.420
GARY: I was going to say that that's a hand grenade.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:40.000
I was once in the US, and someone
ordered deep-fried cheesecake.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.040
Ooo.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.120
GARY: How does that work?

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:45.820
You get a slab of cheesecake,
you batter it, and you deep fry it.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:47.640
And then you add like, raspberry sauce on it.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.520
I had one bite, and was like:
That's lovely, and that is now enough.

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:52.740
[Laughter]

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.320
I did have some cake the other day,

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:59.860
where somebody, rather than creating icing,
had just rendered down entire Mars bars.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:00.680
ALL: Ohhh...

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:03.340
So every element of the Mars bar then re-set into...

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:04.700
TOM: I can't get behind that.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:07.640
When I was... Quite a while ago,
when I had a house in York.

00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:11.660
The woman was a bit dicky but I wanted
to make a cake, right, to take to work..

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:15.160
So I made cake one...
Didn't really rise, door not shutting properly.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:18.320
Now what I should have done is said,
F*** it, I'm going to Tesco.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.820
But damn it, I'd started, I was going to do this.

00:03:20.820 --> 00:03:24.200
So I tried again — that one, also flat.
Tried again, that one also flat.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.200
Oh God, I know where this is going.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:27.940
Third one burnt down, fell over,
and then sank into the swamp.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:29.860
[Laughter]
'But the last one stood up!'

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:31.040
So I put...

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.940
At this point something happened
in my brain that wasn't normal sense.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:37.440
I thought: stack them all together,
put jam and cream in between,

00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:40.260
And then I melted over the top an entire Yorkie.

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:41.280
TOM: [Groans]

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:44.760
In fact, three entire Yorkies,
I think it was, I put over the top of it.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.460
You've just got a brick!
You've got...

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.080
You've got a prohibited weapon there!

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:53.240
I've got to say, it was almost starting
to collapse in on itself, mass-wise.

00:03:53.360 --> 00:03:54.240
GARY: Superdense...

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:57.960
I nearly created a cake black hole, it turns out later on.

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:00.460
Someone said their teeth hurt after eating it.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:01.680
TOM: Yes!

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.120
You've got four cakes crushed down!

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:07.320
'We're out of osmium, best use cake!'

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:08.540
It's diamond.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:13.260
The middle of it was a solid diamond, and small other buns were starting to get trapped in its orbit.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.220
[Laughter]

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.660
Just walking past a cake shop to take it to work,
and stuff's battering up against the glass.

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:21.080
[Whooshing noises]

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:22.900
Cake-based gravity...

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:27.360
Could...? I was gonna say "Could you do that?"
I'm pretty sure the answer is: no.

00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.420
No!

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.440
I'm achieving it as the years pass.

00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:31.300
GARY: Yeah...!

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:33.620
A long time ago we were talking about Lisa Clayton.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:35.060
GARY: Oh yeah, we were, yeah!
TOM: To give her full name...

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:36.180
Did she go into space?

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:40.900
Er, no. Lisa Lyttelton, Dowager Viscountess Cobham.
As she is now known.

00:04:41.060 --> 00:04:43.260
Now that's one for your exam paper, isn't it.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:47.760
Yep. She set out September 1994,
came back June 1995.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.400
Tried to discover America;
worked out it had already been done.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:52.600
[Laughter]
Oh. You know what, you're very close.

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:53.760
Oh f*** off.

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:55.880
CHRIS: And you said 285 days...
TOM: Yup.

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:57.780
Only person to walk across Canada?

00:04:58.060 --> 00:05:01.520
But not seen. People'd see you in Canada.
There are people.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.080
She tried to row across the Atlantic
on her own or something?

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:05.700
Close enough, I'll give you the point. [DING]

00:05:05.700 --> 00:05:09.220
'Fastest Sail Around the World by a Woman,
Single-Handed Without Assistance'.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.200
'First British Woman to Sail Single-Handed
and Non-Stop Around the World'.

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:14.040
Respect!

00:05:14.220 --> 00:05:16.740
Well done to you! That's good work, that is. If you still...

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:19.980
And to be sat there with one of your hands
just in your pocket!

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.920
What?
Single-handed... Ohh.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.000
Yeah, just making it look good!

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:26.940
TOM: Wow...

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.600
That seems like an unnecessary thing to do to yourself.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:30.420
...encumbrance, isn't it, yeah.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.520
There'll be a point someone off the Cape of Good Hope,

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.720
You're like, 'I should really have done this
two-handed, but you know...'

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.320
'I did say at the start!'

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:38.940
MATT: 'An award's an award!'

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.180
A 38-foot yacht. Does anyone want
to take a guess what it was called,

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:46.920
given that she was sponsored by her university?

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:48.300
University of Birmingham.

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:50.680
The Brummie Bastard.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:53.200
It's a good name for a boat.
Not quite.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.120
I was hoping it was sponsored by Staffordshire University, and then she could have been Keelehauled.

00:05:57.320 --> 00:05:59.320
ALL: AYYY!

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:04.260
Keele is a town in Staffordshire.

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:07.520
Was it
[Brummie accent] the 'Alroight'?

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.880
You know what, that's close enough.
It's the Spirit of Birmingham.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.780
I was going to say, HRH Lenny Henry.

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:17.300
GARY: [Laughing] Spirit of Birmingham...

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.160
I like the way you went with *HRH* Lenny Henry...

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:23.040
something I'd like to see —
rather than HMS.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:24.740
[Laughter]

00:06:25.460 --> 00:06:26.320
Me too.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:33.160
31,000-mile journey, single-handed,
round the world, in a yacht...

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.940
Some of the difficulties she'd have had on the way?

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:38.500
Crying. If Ellen MacArthur's anything to go by.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:39.780
Yes, if Ellen MacArthur's anything to go by.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.320
Tesco delivery not turning up.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:44.680
To be honest, you have enough
trouble with that anywhere.

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:49.520
She's just sitting there, sailing around,
trimming the sail...

00:06:49.700 --> 00:06:51.940
All of a sudden, she looks around:

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:55.220
'Sorry we missed you' —
'I'm sitting right here! How did you manage that?!'

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:59.040
'I have a hundred miles of visibility in every direction!'

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:01.940
That's because they've got a Tesco submarine.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:04.980
I like the idea of Tesco submarine delivery...

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.060
because it implies there's a Walmart submarine
from a different superpower steadily hunting them.

00:07:09.200 --> 00:07:13.620
And all Asda can afford is a destroyer on the surface,
hoping for the best with depth charges.

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.940
And there's a Carrefour flotilla coming up...

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.560
'We're loading in the granary torpedoes now!'

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:19.900
Eyyy!

00:07:20.380 --> 00:07:22.600
Granary torpedoes: type of bread.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:23.920
CHRIS: I enjoyed it.
TOM: He got it.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:24.880
I got it.

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:27.160
Hardships on the way, as you're
circumnavigating round the world?

00:07:27.300 --> 00:07:29.220
TOM: What might your boat do?

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:30.140
Fall over!

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:31.680
Yes. Have a point. [DING]

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:33.780
I was going to go for, more drastically: sink.

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:34.640
[Laughter]

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.620
Well, she wouldn't have made it round if it sank.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:40.040
She might have... been able to reinflate it?

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:42.860
TOM: It's not an inflatable dinghy! It's not...

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.980
₤9.99 Tesco summer range inflatable dinghy.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:48.560
Stood on one end, inflating the other end...

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:50.180
'F***in' hell!'

00:07:50.620 --> 00:07:54.340
It's not the Scarborough boating lake
she's trying to circumnavigate here.

00:07:54.520 --> 00:07:57.920
No, she went round that enough times
to make up 31,000 miles.

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:58.900
Yeah. Yeah.

00:07:59.100 --> 00:08:00.280
Capsized seven times.

00:08:00.440 --> 00:08:01.540
GARY: How?
MATT: That's all right.

00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:03.580
I don't know how you right a boat
on your own when it's capsized, but...

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:05.560
MATT: Carefully.
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:10.540
Basically, you stand on the rail, and I think
there's a line that attaches to the end of the mast.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:15.580
I mean, this is the principle for dinghies,
I don't know about a 38-foot yacht.

00:08:15.740 --> 00:08:17.100
TOM: Oh, okay. Yeah.

00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:20.080
But basically you haul on that end of it...

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:23.220
Wow. And then you go back in the water as it...

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:25.460
MATT: And hope it doesn't keep going.
TOM: Yeah.

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:27.860
Yeah. That's the principle with smaller vessels.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:31.640
I don't know if her weight's enough to do it,
or whether she can move ballast across in...

00:08:31.820 --> 00:08:33.260
Ah, that's a point, yeah.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.880
It's probably water-based ballast,
so you pump it up to the topside or something.

00:08:36.880 --> 00:08:39.440
Or the side that's out of the water,
and then you lean on it...

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:42.860
I would have thought you wouldn't go all the way
around the world with a boat you couldn't right.

00:08:43.020 --> 00:08:46.340
Because it's capsized, then you've got 'turtle' which
is when the mast's downwards, and then you just...

00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:48.240
GARY: That's it, she's had it.
CHRIS: You go home.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.280
CHRIS: On the other hand, did you see this thing,
it was earlier in the year...

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.960
You know there are now these carbon trimarans
with solid carbon sails.

00:08:55.960 --> 00:08:58.220
It's just like a big vane on the back.

00:08:58.220 --> 00:08:59.620
They flipped one.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:00.860
MATT: [Gasps]
GARY: Oh, I can see that...

00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:02.140
They nosed in.

00:09:02.260 --> 00:09:03.900
Oh, is this the America's Cup?

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:05.800
The America's Cup one. Yeah.

00:09:05.940 --> 00:09:07.380
TOM: That killed a man.
MATT: That killed them. Yeah.

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:12.720
Because the new ones are essentially hydrofoils,
floating above for most of the journey.

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:13.540
CHRIS: Yeah.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:15.380
Fifty miles an hour, carbon fibre...

00:09:15.500 --> 00:09:17.560
Nosed in, flipped it — mast down.

00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:18.440
GARY: Yikes.

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:25.240
So the America's Cup works by the winner of the
previous America's Cup sets the rules for the next lot.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:26.860
Oh, is that it? Ahhh.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:28.880
TOM: And the person who won the last one was...

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.280
I think it was a billionaire who runs Oracle
and all sorts of IT groups.

00:09:31.400 --> 00:09:32.820
Yeah, that's it. It was Oracle.

00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:36.240
So he said, all the boats — carbon fibre, high speed...

00:09:36.380 --> 00:09:37.360
CHRIS: Trimaran...
TOM: Yeah.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.860
Cool things. Essentially, if Batman had a sailing boat...

00:09:41.060 --> 00:09:41.560
Yeah.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:43.160
[Gravelly Batman voice]
'To the Bat-Boat!'

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:45.380
TOM: But when they crash... it's terrifying.
MATT: Everyone dies.

00:09:45.540 --> 00:09:48.260
Yeah. Well, only one. Everyone else was sort of injured.

00:09:48.400 --> 00:09:49.300
Oh really? I thought it was everyone.

00:09:49.460 --> 00:09:53.320
No, no. They were rattled but they managed to
limp it back to port or get it towed or something.

00:09:53.460 --> 00:09:57.280
God, that'd be great in other competitions,
if the winner could set the rules for next time.

00:09:57.280 --> 00:10:01.400
Boxing! You can have the springy boxing gloves
that come out like that and back in.

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:06.440
And until you beat me using that methodology,
I'm not interested.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:08.860
We need to get Elon Musk to win tennis.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.760
'Yep. You have to do it in space.'

00:10:11.120 --> 00:10:12.640
You mentioned Dame Ellen MacArthur, by the way...

00:10:12.820 --> 00:10:14.080
GARY: Yeah.
MATT: Oh yeah.

00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.320
And it's quite interesting to note the difference between her and Lisa Clayton...

00:10:17.320 --> 00:10:21.120
Obviously, Ellen MacArthur —
knighthood for breaking the record...

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.800
Lisa Clayton has essentially slipped into obscurity.

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:25.340
Well, not anymore!

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:26.300
TOM: [Laughs]

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.520
All eight of you watching, please spread the word!

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:32.160
At the end of the show, congratulations Matt,
you're this week's winner!

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:35.540
Yeeeeeeeeees!

00:10:36.000 --> 00:10:37.820
That went on for much longer than I expected it to!

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.420
But slightly less long than I was expecting.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:41.420
Yeah. Yeah.

00:10:41.600 --> 00:10:45.880
You win a British Prime Minister's feeding trough
that tells kids to be wary of people they don't know.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:48.540
It's John Major's Stranger Danger Manger.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:49.640
Heyyy!

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:51.280
Enjoy that —

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:52.520
I've got a use for one of them, actually.

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.340
In the meantime —
I don't want to know why.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:57.000
TOM: That's been Chris Joel.
CHRIS: Godspeed.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:10:58.560
TOM: That's been Gary Brannan...
GARY: Godspeed.

00:10:58.660 --> 00:10:59.640
TOM: That's been Matt Gray...
MATT: Bye bye.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.280
I've been Tom Scott, and we'll see you next time.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:11.940
Is this something like Fish Slapping Dance?

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:13.540
ALL: Ohh!

00:11:13.820 --> 00:11:15.340
With a side o' bacon...

00:11:15.580 --> 00:11:16.620
Bacon beating!

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.000
Brannan, if you'd care to mime with me...

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:18.920
GARY: Go on.

00:11:20.780 --> 00:11:21.700
Comme ça?

00:11:23.300 --> 00:11:24.900
Bradford Crane, which —

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:26.120
Bradford! [Laughs]

00:11:26.640 --> 00:11:27.760
Bradford Crane.

00:11:27.760 --> 00:11:29.820
'Hello? Bradford Cranes?'

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:35.360
'Er, yeah, we got a 9-tonne, a 14-tonne,
and er, Maurice Micklewhite...'

